{"author":"Joe Oakhart","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"content_hash":"46efde36fe8d828b8c784d0a7a025477dea0fe4e62e8dbb89cabb55bb7f83532","digest":{"component_or_area":"transition/close gate and documentation","key_entities":["src/rebar/_lib_writes.py","src/rebar/_commands/transition.py","src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py","docs/concurrency.md","force flag","unresolved-children guard","claim gates"],"problem_keywords":["documentation","force flag semantics","gate bypass","ticket invariants","close restriction","inconsistency"],"propositions":["Public Python documentation describes force as waiving the unresolved-children close restriction, but CLI and implementation enforce that restriction unconditionally regardless of force.","The force flag should bypass all gates (including future gates) but not ticket-system invariants like the prohibition on closing a parent with open children.","Documentation inconsistently defines force semantics across Python library, CLI, MCP, and user guidance surfaces.","The open-children close prohibition must be classified as a ticket-system invariant rather than a gate to clarify what force can and cannot bypass."]},"digest_hash_version":1,"model":"bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-opus-4-8","schema":"ticket_digest_v1"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"TICKET_DIGEST","timestamp":1787543405905778001,"uuid":"05aa5e6c-a740-4724-93d3-56bc5d5b04ca"}